.TH GETOPT 1
.SH NAME
getopt \- normalize short options into shell-quoted arguments
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B getopt
optstring [argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Parse classic short options. A colon after an option letter requires a value.
Clustered flags and attached values are accepted. Parsing stops at the first
non-option or an explicit --. Output contains each parsed option, its value,
a -- separator and remaining operands, all quoted for the Bourne shell.
Spaces, empty arguments, apostrophes and shell punctuation are preserved.
No argument vector is written if parsing or allocation fails.
.PP
Use eval to interpret the quotes emitted by getopt, not unquoted set alone.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
args=`getopt ab: "$@"` || exit 1
eval "set -- $args"
.fi
.SH LIMITS
This is the classic OPTSTRING interface, not GNU getopt. Long options,
optional option values and operand permutation are not provided.
The invoking shell and exec argument/environment budget still apply.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on option/allocation/output error, 2 when optstring is missing.
